<p class="lead">Most course businesses and training teams start on an off-the-shelf learning platform, and that's the right call. At some point the workarounds pile up. Here is how to tell when custom LMS development makes sense, what it involves, and how to move without losing anything.</p>
<h2 id="signs-youve-outgrown-your-lms">Signs you've outgrown your LMS</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Staff run key processes in spreadsheets because the platform can't do them.</li>
  <li>Your pricing, such as cohorts, company seats or bundles, doesn't fit the platform's model.</li>
  <li>Per-user fees grow faster than your revenue.</li>
  <li>You need integrations the platform doesn't offer: your CRM, HR system or payments.</li>
  <li>The learning experience you want, such as live cohorts, coaching or AI practice, isn't possible.</li>
  <li>You don't fully own or control your learner data.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-a-custom-lms-involves">What a custom LMS involves</h2>
<p>The core is smaller than it looks:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Users, roles and organizations</li>
  <li>Courses, modules and content, including video, documents and quizzes</li>
  <li>Progress tracking and completion rules</li>
  <li>Assessments and certificates</li>
  <li>Payments and access control</li>
  <li>Reporting for admins and clients</li>
</ul>
<p>Add standards only if you need them. SCORM or xAPI matter if you import packaged courses from authoring tools. LTI matters if schools or partners need to plug your content into their own LMS.</p>
<h2 id="build-in-stages">Build in stages</h2>
<p>Start with what makes your offer different and what the current platform blocks. Launch that to a pilot group, then add the rest. Our <a href="/services/mvp-development">MVP development</a> approach works this way: a working product in milestones, not a long build before anyone uses it.</p>
<h2 id="middle-options">Middle options</h2>
<p>You don't always have to replace everything. Some teams keep their LMS for content and build a custom portal around it for sign-up, pricing, reporting or coaching. Others connect a custom app to the LMS through LTI.</p>
<h2 id="moving-without-losing-data">Moving without losing data</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Export users, enrolments, progress, grades and certificates.</li>
  <li>Map them to the new data model and test with a copy first.</li>
  <li>Keep old course URLs redirecting to the new ones.</li>
  <li>Run both platforms for one cohort before switching everyone.</li>
</ol>
